course: dictionary
lesson: 4912
title: Imitation: a copy, a fake, or copying
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Imitation» can mean a copy of something real, or copying someone's voice or actions. Look at the words around it to choose.
explain: When the adjective «imitation» comes before a thing, it tells you the thing is not real. «Imitation leather» looks like leather but is made from something else. Shops use this meaning for cheaper copies, such as imitation gold. Ask yourself: is this the real thing, or a copy made to look like it?
explain: In drama and games, an imitation is copying someone's voice or actions, often to be funny. An imitation of a teacher makes the class laugh because it is a copy of her, not the real teacher. The saying «Imitation is the sincerest form of flattery» means that copying someone shows you admire them.
fact: Imitation can be a noun (an imitation, imitations) or an adjective (imitation leather).
fact: «Imitation» means a copy of something, often a fake that looks like the real thing.
fact: «Imitation» can also mean copying someone's voice or actions, often to be funny.
fact: In 1950 Turing suggested the «imitation game», a test of whether a person chatting by text can tell a machine from a human.
fact: A cheap imitation is a copy that costs less than the real thing, such as imitation jewellery.
exercise: «The shop sold imitation gold rings for a few pounds.» What does imitation mean here? | Real gold, sold at a low price | Gold that has been stolen | A shiny ring with a jewel in it | *A copy that looks like real gold but is not real
exercise: Mia can do an imitation of her teacher's voice that makes the whole class laugh. What does imitation mean here? | *Copying someone's voice, often to be funny | Telling a true story about a teacher | Writing a letter to the head teacher | Singing a song in a choir
exercise: The saying «Imitation is the sincerest form of flattery» suggests that copying someone means you | are bored of them | *admire them | are angry with them | want to forget them
test: «In 1950 Turing suggested the "imitation game".» In this sentence, what is the imitation game? | A card game played with pictures | A game where children copy each other's dance moves | *A test of whether a person chatting by text can tell a machine from a human | A kind of leather
test: In which phrase is imitation an adjective, describing something that is not real? | do an imitation of | *imitation jewellery | a cheap imitation | imitation is the sincerest form of flattery
exam: «The fake watch was an imitation of a famous brand.» Which meaning fits best? | A test for computers | *A copy that looks like the real thing | Copying someone's voice to be funny | A kind of animal
